---
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const starterConfig = `version = 1

[discovery]
extendExclude = []
useDefaultExcludes = true
respectGitIgnore = true
forceExclude = false
followSymlinks = false

[schemas]
maxDepth = 8
concurrency = 8
requireCoverage = false

[schemas.optimizations]
enabled = true

[schemas.optimizations.azure]
pruneResources = true

[schemas.fetch]
enabled = true
timeout = "10s"
retries = 2
retryMinWait = "250ms"
retryMaxWait = "2s"
allowedDomains = []
blockedDomains = []

[schemas.compile]
timeout = "30s"

[schemas.catalogs]
enabled = true
failure = "warn"

[[schemas.catalogs.sources]]
name = "schemastore"
format = "schemastore"
url = "https://www.schemastore.org/api/json/catalog.json"
enabled = true

[[schemas.associations]]
file = "settings/*.toml"
schema = "./schemas/settings.schema.json"

[output]
showSkipped = false
verbose = false
quiet = false
locations = false
branchErrors = "best"

[[ignore]]
file = "fixtures/*.json"
keyword = "required"
property = "legacyName"
reason = "legacy fixture kept for compatibility"`;

const associationConfig = `[[schemas.associations]]
file = "settings/*.toml"
schema = "./schemas/settings.schema.json"`;

const ignoreConfig = `[[ignore]]
file = "fixtures/*.json"
keyword = "required"
property = "legacyName"
reason = "legacy fixture kept for compatibility"`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Configuration reference" current="/reference/configuration/">
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="doc-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">Reference</span>
      <h1>Configuration</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        dollarlint reads TOML config from <code>.dollarlint.toml</code>.
        Directory validation searches the target root; explicit file validation
        searches beside the file. Use <code>--config</code> to point at a specific
        config.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>Starter config</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={starterConfig} language="toml" />
    <p>
      Format and output file are per-run choices — use <code>--format</code> and <code
        >--output</code
      > on <code>dollarlint validate</code> whenever you need JSON, SARIF, or a file
      artifact.
    </p>

    <h2>Discovery</h2>
    <p>
      Leave <code>include</code> unset to use the built-in JSON, YAML, YML, and TOML
      discovery defaults. Set it only when you want to replace that default set with
      custom discovery globs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Discovery, exclude, association, and ignore-file globs are matched against
      slash-separated paths relative to the validation root. A glob without a
      slash matches basenames at any depth, so <code>*.json</code> matches both <code
        >package.json</code
      > and <code>config/settings.json</code>. Use slashes when you want to
      match a particular relative path shape.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Key</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>include</code></td><td
            >Optional replacement discovery globs to consider. Repeatable CLI
            includes replace config includes for that run.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>extendExclude</code></td><td
            >Additional discovery globs to ignore, on top of the built-in
            default excludes.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>useDefaultExcludes</code></td><td
            >Skip common VCS, dependency, generated, cache, and virtual
            environment directories.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>respectGitIgnore</code></td><td
            >Apply the root <code>.gitignore</code> during directory discovery.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>forceExclude</code></td><td
            >Apply excludes even to explicitly passed files. By default,
            explicit file targets bypass excludes.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>followSymlinks</code></td><td
            >Follow symbolic links during discovery.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Default excludes are intentionally not written out by <code
        >dollarlint init</code
      >. Keep project-specific ignores in <code>extendExclude</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Schemas</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Key</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>maxDepth</code></td><td
            >Maximum external schema reference depth.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>concurrency</code></td><td
            >Number of concurrent validation workers. Defaults to <code
              >GOMAXPROCS</code
            >.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>requireCoverage</code></td><td
            >Fail when a discovered included file has no in-file schema, config
            association, built-in association, or catalog match.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Associations</h2>
    <p>
      Associations map discovered files to schemas when the file does not
      declare one itself. dollarlint also has a built-in association for <code
        >.dollarlint.toml</code
      >, backed by the embedded config schema; explicit in-file declarations and
      user associations still win.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={associationConfig} language="toml" />
    <p>
      Association globs are matched against paths relative to the validation
      root and use the same basename-any-depth behavior as discovery globs.
      In-file declarations win over associations.
    </p>

    <h2>Fetch and compile</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Key</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.fetch.enabled</code></td><td
            >Allow fetching HTTP schemas and catalogs.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.fetch.timeout</code></td><td
            >Timeout for each schema fetch.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.fetch.retries</code></td><td
            >Retries for transient remote schema fetch failures. <code>408</code
            >, <code>425</code>, <code>429</code>, and retryable <code>5xx</code
            > responses are retried.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.fetch.retryMinWait</code></td><td
            >Minimum retry backoff.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.fetch.retryMaxWait</code></td><td
            >Maximum retry backoff.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.fetch.allowedDomains</code></td><td
            >Restrict remote schemas to exact or wildcard hosts. For
            SchemaStore, use <code>*.schemastore.org</code> or include both <code
              >www.schemastore.org</code
            > and <code>json.schemastore.org</code>.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.fetch.blockedDomains</code></td><td
            >Deny hosts even when they match the allowlist.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.compile.timeout</code></td><td
            >Timeout for compiling each schema.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Optimizations</h2>
    <p>
      Optimizations reduce expensive schema work without changing validation
      semantics. They are enabled by default and can be turned off globally or
      one optimization at a time when you need to debug schema behavior exactly
      as fetched.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Key</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.optimizations.enabled</code></td><td
            >Enable or disable schema-specific optimizations as a group.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.optimizations.azure.pruneResources</code></td><td
            >For Azure Resource Manager deployment schemas from <code
              >schema.management.azure.com</code
            >, compile only the resource provider schemas referenced by the
            template instead of the full Azure provider catalog. This keeps
            ordinary ARM template validation much faster. Set it to <code
              >false</code
            > if you need to inspect or debug the unpruned schema.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Catalogs</h2>
    <p>
      Catalogs map well-known filenames to schemas when a file doesn't declare <code
        >$schema</code
      > and no explicit or built-in association matches. SchemaStore is the built-in
      catalog source, and custom sources can use the same catalog shape.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Key</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.catalogs.enabled</code></td><td
            >Enable catalog filename matching.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.catalogs.failure</code></td><td
            >Choose <code>warn</code>, <code>error</code>, or <code>skip</code> when
            a catalog cannot be loaded.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.catalogs.sources</code></td><td
            >List SchemaStore-format catalog sources.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.catalogs.sources[].name</code></td><td
            >Human-readable source name used in schema source metadata and
            warnings.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.catalogs.sources[].format</code></td><td
            >Catalog format. Currently <code>schemastore</code>.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.catalogs.sources[].url</code></td><td
            >Remote catalog URL. Mutually exclusive with <code>path</code>.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.catalogs.sources[].path</code></td><td
            >Local catalog path. Mutually exclusive with <code>url</code>.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>schemas.catalogs.sources[].enabled</code></td><td
            >Disable one source without deleting it.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Ignore rules</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={ignoreConfig} language="toml" />
    <p>
      Ignore rules match validation issues by file glob, JSON Schema keyword,
      and property. All configured fields must match; omitted fields act like
      wildcards. Ignored issues are still reported as ignored and do not make
      the run fail.
    </p>

    <h2>Output preferences</h2>
    <p>
      Config can set issue-reporting and text-output preferences. Machine-readable
      formats and output files are intentionally per-run choices.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Key</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr
          ><td><code>output.showSkipped</code></td><td
            >Show skipped files in text output.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>output.verbose</code></td><td
            >Show expanded issue metadata in text output.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>output.quiet</code></td><td>Use terse success output.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>output.locations</code></td><td
            >Include line and column positions in text and JSON output.</td
          ></tr
        >
        <tr
          ><td><code>output.branchErrors</code></td><td
            >Choose <code>best</code> to report the closest matching <code
              >oneOf</code
            >/<code>anyOf</code> branch, or <code>all</code> to show every failed
            branch leaf when debugging complex schemas.</td
          ></tr
        >
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
